      <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
During the blower door test, we identified that your house is reasonably air tight, however there are a few specific air leakage (heat loss) locations that will be eliminated in conjunction with your planned renovations &amp; mechanical equipment upgrades:

** The 6" chimney for the current oil furnace.

** The older windows.

** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, and the attic access panel

Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you can achieve about a 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 74 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 and we discussed your interest to add more insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).  I have modeled adding R15, bringing the total R-value close to the minimum R30 that is generally required for new construction in the NWT. If you decide not to add insulation to the wall that adjoins the garage, this would reduce the rebate by 17%.

Savings: By increasing 83% of the exterior walls by a minimum R15, you could potentially save $ 719 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 4,150 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), Please note that a section of your foundation walls is above ground and would also have some exterior insulation added in conjunction with the main floor walls (refer to that section of this report). I have incorporated the energy savings into the numbers above, however there would be an addition $450 rebate, bringing the total "wall rebate" to $4,600.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>
The foundation walls of your house are insulated on both the interior (2x4 R12) and on the exterior (2" of expanded polystyrene foam board = R8). The basement is 7.75' tall, with about 3.5' of the "above grade" portion having an exterior siding finish the same as the upper main floor walls. In conjunction with your plans to add exterior insulation to the main floor walls, you will also extend this down to cover the upper section of the foundation walls - I have therefore modeled the addition of 3" (R15) of rigid foam board insulation to this section of wall (excluding the section of wall that adjoins the garage). The calculated foundation wall area that would receive an insulation upgrade is 36% of the total foundation wall area.

Savings: By adding R15 to the 3.5' of above grade foundation wall (not including the section adjoined to the garage), the energy savings are included in the "main walls" upgrade recommendation outlined in this report, however you would be eligible for an additional $450 in rebates (36% x $625 x 200%) from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>
Your current windows are older and you are planning to upgrade them. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all the main floor &amp; basement windows (10 in total) with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.

Savings: 
By replacing these 10 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 601 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.4 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 

To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program
</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 175 litre electric hot water tank. We talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 691 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an older oil furnace (estimated 83% efficiency). In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from the oil furnace to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 637 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 31.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>
Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:
        - a 450 CFM range hood       
        - two bathroom fans
        - a clothes dryer

There is also a fresh air supply duct connected to furnace ductwork to bring in some outside fresh air each time the furnace operates and whenever one of the above exhaust fans is operating. There is a manual damper on this fresh air that you can adjust to determine how much fresh air enters the house throughout the year.

Your house appears to currently have adequate ventilation, however you will find it a bit tighter after you add wall insulation (including a new air barrier wrap), install new windows &amp; upgrade the furnace (eliminating the 6" chimney). If you find the indoor air a bit too humid or perhaps in need of more fresh air, you may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that incorporates programmable operation or has a built-in humidity sensor.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 16.5 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace vents up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Fort Smith

The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:
Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.25 per kWh
Oil  - $1.17 per litre 
Propane - $0.87 per litre
Wood - $242 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $8.49 per bag, $288.46 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333
Fax: (867) 873-0303
E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climah transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
